WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:07.460
thank you all for joining us at this special
CNN keep watching screening event.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:13.180
I'm dr Tara Narula CNN medical correspondent
and I'm so thrilled to be joined by Dr Fabi

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:20.250
Hirsch Cruise Ceo and director of clinical
services at Friends of Aphasia. What an incredible

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.430
film and story Dr Hirsch. So let's jump right
in. I want to begin with having you describe

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:31.679
for us the very first time that you met then
Representative Gabby Giffords. Yeah. So I

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:38.989
met her for the first time in her home. She
and her husband Mark Kelly had invited me

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:45.489
over really to interview for a speech pathology
position to work with Gabby. She had just

00:00:45.489 --> 00:00:53.050
returned from Houston where she was receiving
therapy services there to come back to Tucson.

00:00:53.050 --> 00:01:00.149
She was very excited to come back and I was
invited to meet her and see if it would be

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:07.250
a good fit. And so I went to her home and
I remember I was seated at the kitchen table

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:14.299
and she came in, she was singing, she walked
right over, sat across from me and just reached

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:22.920
out and took my hand and just just looked
at me with this beautiful beaming smile and

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:30.100
I knew right away that I definitely wanted
to be part of her rehabilitation rehabilitation

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:36.290
process. And um I felt like it was going to
be a good match and it really has been a good

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:47.140
fit. It's been a really special team for 13
years. Well of course we about about 10 years.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:53.189
So it's been 10 years that the two of you
have been working so closely together and

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:59.719
of course we We all remember the horrific
mass shooting that injured representative

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:05.190
Giffords and nearly took her life. Six people
were tragically killed that day. 13 people

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.060
were wounded. For those in our viewing audience.
Can you give us a brief description of the

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.470
kinds of brain injuries that she sustained
and specifically the kind of injury that resulted

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:26.120
in her needing your care? So the gunshot wound
through Gabby's brain really hit just those

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:31.150
speech and language areas that she needs for
communication. So much of the rest of her

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:39.310
brain was spared and that's why she really
relies on speech and language services to

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:46.440
help with her rehabilitation. I rarely see
people following a gunshot wound to the head

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.500
because most people don't survive. So Gabby
is incredibly remarkable in her story, in

00:02:53.500 --> 00:03:01.769
her recovery because she is you have such
a fighter and continues to improve. And so

00:03:01.769 --> 00:03:07.549
most of the people you treat if it's not from
brain injuries that are traumatic from a gunshot,

00:03:07.549 --> 00:03:13.720
for example, what are the kinds of causes
of injuries that can result in speech difficulties

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:23.569
or language issues? Yeah, so for aphasia,
specifically the most common cause is stroke.

00:03:23.569 --> 00:03:30.349
So about 80% of people who are living with
aphasia have it as a result of a stroke, but

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:40.069
other causes can also result in aphasia. So
brain tumor or an infection or brain degeneration,

00:03:40.069 --> 00:03:47.040
anything that can impact the language areas
of the brain can result in aphasia and so

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:54.020
many people don't know what aphasia is. Can
you tell us? How do you define Aphasia? Yes.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:01.880
So aphasia is the loss of the ability or at
least part of the ability to use words to

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:10.239
communicate. And often we think of communication
just from the perspective of speech or speaking.

00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:16.049
And certainly people who have Aphasia have
difficulty with speaking. We also need to

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:21.900
take information in when we're communicating
with others, so comprehending what others

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:30.240
are saying and written communications. So
we write to express ourselves. We read to

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:38.031
take information in all of those areas. Speaking,
understanding reading and writing can all

00:04:38.031 --> 00:04:45.720
be impacted by Aphasia. A really important
thing though to keep in mind is that Aphasia

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:54.371
impacts language and communication. It does
not impact intelligence or cognition. Thinking

00:04:54.371 --> 00:05:02.751
skills strong as ever. It really impacts the
ability to use language to communicate. Which

00:05:02.751 --> 00:05:09.350
I think is almost doubly frustrating because
people who have Aphasia know exactly what

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:16.280
they want to say. But it's such a struggle
to get those words out. And your group actually

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:23.380
has a special bracelet that you wear. What
are the words inside that bracelet. So I would

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.630
be people would not be happy if I wasn't wearing
my bracelet. So we do have our friends, we

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:35.210
have our friends of Aphasia bracelet, but
with so few people knowing what Aphasia is.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:46.289
We also have the definition on the inside.
So it says aphasia loss of words not intelligence.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:50.840
Such an important point to make and thank
you for explaining that I want to go back

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:56.889
to Gabby. Are there particular challenges?
Uh in her instance for example to recovering

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:01.870
from aphasia, what does the trajectory of
recovery look like? How long can it take?

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:12.010
What is the prognosis? Yeah, so everyone is
very different in how they are living with

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:21.900
Aphasia, just the severity or if it's reading
is more difficult or speaking. And for Gabby

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:30.530
specifically she does have difficulty in each
of the language areas I mentioned. So speaking

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:38.590
is certainly difficult for her to come towards
easily to build those words into sentences.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:45.000
She does have some real strengths like remembering
people's names, which can often be really

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.780
difficult for all of us. She is masterful
at remembering people's names. I rely on her

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:59.669
for that. Um but also she has some struggles
taking information in sometimes. So it's helpful

00:06:59.669 --> 00:07:06.349
um you know to to be able to really see people
when they're interacting with her is is very

00:07:06.349 --> 00:07:13.259
helpful. Um and reading and writing are tricky
as well, so she works really hard in all of

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:20.919
those areas because they're so important to
her. But she's also very wise in using strategies

00:07:20.919 --> 00:07:28.740
to support. So for instance when she wants
to read something from the paper, she also

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:35.310
listens to it on her ipad. So she gets the
information in both by listening and by reading

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:42.050
and she's just very good at using these techniques
to help ensure that she's getting the full

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:50.169
message as far as oh, I was just gonna say
as far as the trajectory of recovery, she

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:59.970
continues to improve. And I do worry that
in the medical community and just in the communities

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:09.160
in general, there is this notion that there's
a plateau that somehow after six months or

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:17.280
12 months or 18 months, that there will be
no more progress and that certainly has not

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:24.300
been my experience with Gabby with all of
the people I work with at our Aphasia Center,

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:32.529
people continue to improve and there may be
areas maybe writing is kind of on a not showing

00:08:32.529 --> 00:08:39.099
those kinds of gains for a bit because our
brains need time to consolidate information.

00:08:39.099 --> 00:08:45.339
You know, you learn a new skill, you need
time for your brain to really anchor that

00:08:45.339 --> 00:08:52.779
skill and then it can start growing again.
So unfortunately in our health care system,

00:08:52.779 --> 00:09:00.520
we often look at those times where it feels
like a plateau, but it's really just a time

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:08.040
where our brain is consolidating information
and getting ready to work on showing some

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:15.060
gains again. So it's, it's important that
people realize and Gabby is such a remarkable

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:23.320
example of that, that recovery doesn't stop
with good support. It can continue to go on

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:30.519
and on and on. And we certainly see that.
And the two of you co founded a nonprofit

00:09:30.519 --> 00:09:36.709
friends of Aphasia why? Uh and what are some
of the other misconceptions around aphasia,

00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:42.870
You mentioned the concern with intelligence
and explained that are there any other misconceptions

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:51.850
that individuals with Aphasia, that people
perceive them differently? Because so few

00:09:51.850 --> 00:10:00.190
people know what aphasia is when they interact
with someone who has aphasia, they often can't

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:08.470
quite make sense of of what is causing the
communication difficulty. And just as an example,

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:17.019
so most of our members will carry a card that
says they have aphasia so that if they are

00:10:17.019 --> 00:10:24.320
in a situation, for example, we've had members
who have gotten into fender benders and when

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:32.340
the other driver or the police arrive to be
able to show that they have aphasia and that's

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:39.740
why they're struggling to communicate will
dispel. What is often a first thought is that

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:46.580
the person has been drinking or the person
has been doing drugs and that's why they can't

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:53.639
talk properly. So that's always something
that we worry is that people will think something

00:10:53.639 --> 00:10:59.709
else is causing somebody not to be able to
get to their words. And so really being able

00:10:59.709 --> 00:11:06.540
to let folks know right away. So there isn't
that misconception, that's certainly something

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:13.120
that we, that we worry about. And also we
worry that people will think because people

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:19.410
can't communicate that they don't have those
intact thinking skills, that their intelligence

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:27.200
isn't intact. And it it most certainly is,
it most certainly is. And we see it despite

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:32.910
the communication difficulties every day at
the ASia Center? obviously it's so important

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:37.620
in an individual's recovery to work one on
one with a speech therapist, but talk to me

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:42.430
a little bit about what the benefit is of
being in a group with other individuals who

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:49.251
have Aphasia. What does that do in terms of
support and helping and recovery? Yeah, I

00:11:49.251 --> 00:11:57.640
think for me our Aphasia groups are the most
valuable resource I can offer for people living

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:07.769
with Aphasia. Our Aphasia groups are really
designed to address two goals. So we certainly

00:12:07.769 --> 00:12:14.300
want to work on improving communication skills.
So we have groups that work on word finding,

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:28.329
reading, writing, but a really big second
piece is the camaraderie and support and friendship

00:12:28.329 --> 00:12:36.670
that we foster within our community at the
Aphasia Center and just meeting other people

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:46.070
who are struggling with the same difficulties
can be a huge benefit in the recovery process.

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:51.760
And we have folks there certainly folks who
come to our Aphasia Center who really are

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:57.279
focused on improving their skills and then
there are some who come because it's so fun

00:12:57.279 --> 00:13:04.190
and they have such good friends there and
we really have a beautiful community where

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:08.970
we're all very supportive of one another,
get to know each other and get to share our

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:17.079
experiences without worrying that people won't
understand us. We're very supportive. Um so

00:13:17.079 --> 00:13:24.490
I I love our Aphasia groups and the support
that we can see the group members provide

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:30.370
to each other. It's just wonderful. I definitely
saw that when I visited, it was apparent and

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:34.850
I know all of you hang out outside of group
two on bike rides and hikes and and other

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:41.079
activities. So it's great to see that that
camaraderie um I want to ask you about the

00:13:41.079 --> 00:13:46.310
film and your involvement, what was your initial
reaction to the idea of a film and to the

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:53.560
idea of having therapeutic sessions be filmed
or to kind of reveal that behind the curtain.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:59.959
Look at what that process was like. We always
think about that very intimate patient doctor

00:13:59.959 --> 00:14:06.370
therapist relationship and this was sort of
pulling the curtain back a little bit. Yeah,

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:14.040
they, most of the filming was done during
the pandemic. So Gabby and I spent a lot of

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:19.810
time together on zoom um doing our therapy
sessions, we we were probably able to spend

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:27.130
more time than usual together. Well, virtually
because she didn't have her very busy travel

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:33.009
schedule at that time. Um so we spent a lot
of time together and we recorded all of the

00:14:33.009 --> 00:14:41.960
sessions for gosh, many, many months and I
think initially I was a little concerned because

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:46.480
you know, I'm just sitting in front of a zoom
session with someone, I don't really worry

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:53.050
about anyone else seeing it. It is like you
said that sort of intimate um dynamic just

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:59.110
between you and your patient. Um but then
if you ever do start thinking oh a lot of

00:14:59.110 --> 00:15:05.110
other people could be seeing this. But I think
that passed very quickly. And part of that

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:11.209
is just Gabby's nature. You get drawn into
the session and you're just enjoying your

00:15:11.209 --> 00:15:17.610
time with her. And I don't really think about
the fact that anybody else might be watching

00:15:17.610 --> 00:15:24.230
this I think later I was like oh gosh did
I brush my hair before every session? But

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:34.580
yeah it was I love how they captured really
are are dynamic and especially Gabby's just

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:42.660
compassion and her concern for everybody else.
You know you really feel like oh as a clinician

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:47.220
I should be the one supporting her. And in
the film the very first scene that we're both

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:52.440
in she is showing just what a compassionate
person she is and makes me smile every time

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:57.620
to see that section. Did you think it was
important as well to show some of her struggles

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:07.139
during therapy To give insight into just how
hard and frustrating it can be? I think so

00:16:07.139 --> 00:16:17.329
and I give the directors Betsy and julie a
lot of credit for not just sugarcoating things

00:16:17.329 --> 00:16:25.399
and really demonstrating the struggles that
she has and I think it is important to for

00:16:25.399 --> 00:16:33.930
others to see that because I feel like people
often see a speech that she gives or you know

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:39.430
certainly at the Democratic National Convention
when she gave her speech they think oh she

00:16:39.430 --> 00:16:46.819
has recovered she is fluent and I've heard
people say oh well she just reads it. It is

00:16:46.819 --> 00:16:55.060
certainly not that easy by any means. We spent
almost a year working on the D. N. C. Speech

00:16:55.060 --> 00:17:04.010
And I think it's important for people to see
that for her to give these beautifully fluent

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:12.240
speeches. They start in a place that is really
difficult with and targeting each word and

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:20.110
then each line and then line after line and
just practice and practice and practice. So

00:17:20.110 --> 00:17:26.620
both for just people in general to see the
type of determination and dedication that

00:17:26.620 --> 00:17:34.080
goes into those speeches. But then for people
who are living with Aphasia or any disability

00:17:34.080 --> 00:17:43.620
that the determination can help really get
folks far. And I think that will inspire people

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:51.510
to really work hard and do their best. And
I know Gabby is hopeful that that's how other

00:17:51.510 --> 00:17:58.409
people will leave the film as well. Just feeling
inspired to do their best. And what feedback

00:17:58.409 --> 00:18:06.760
have you gotten so far from people who have
seen the film? Everybody has said they love

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:12.270
it but I'm not sure they would say anything
else to me. I have to say. So I've seen it

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:22.100
several times and when I leave each time I
watch it after the words I always feel so

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:28.700
uplifted. But then I'm always surprised when
I watch it the next time because the first

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:37.309
parts of the film are so difficult like they
you know Mark you know so lovely that Mark

00:18:37.309 --> 00:18:45.270
and Gabby both decided to share early footage
from when she was in the hospital and things

00:18:45.270 --> 00:18:55.280
that are really hard, I think, to see even
for me. And then as the film proceeds. And

00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:00.870
again, I think this is where the directors
were masterful in just taking us from a place

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:11.020
of real tragedy to this, this wonderful life
story. It's it's really beautiful. Do you

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:19.789
know how Gabby Giffords and Mark Kelly feel
about the film? I think they're really happy

00:19:19.789 --> 00:19:25.580
with it. I know I've heard Mark say that he
was told never to let anyone make a documentary

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:33.169
about them. Um but they're I think they're
very pleased with how it all turned out and

00:19:33.169 --> 00:19:44.370
um it's really just to be able to highlight
Gabby's incredible determination and strength

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:52.679
and to be able to show their very unique relationship,
I think also is just something that comes

00:19:52.679 --> 00:19:57.700
to life on the screen and I'm glad people
have the opportunity to see that themselves.

00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:02.200
I was just gonna ask about that because that
is such a big part of the film as well, is

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:07.200
really that love and connection between the
two of them. Um and I wanted to see from your

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:14.159
perspective, what role support that Mark Kelly
gave to Gabby Giffords, What what role did

00:20:14.159 --> 00:20:20.059
that play in her recovery? Because you told
me that you often see people have family members

00:20:20.059 --> 00:20:32.799
pull away actually from them because the communication
is difficult. Yes, so isolation is a very

00:20:32.799 --> 00:20:41.570
big reality for people who have a fazer any
communication difficulty. It's very tempting

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:52.880
to pull back to not go out and communicate
with colleagues, friends, family and also

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:57.870
family members, friends, people who don't
understand of Asia just feel uncomfortable

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:06.760
with a communication struggle will back out
of somebody's life. And so, you know, furthering

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:16.850
that kind of isolation. And Mark really has
just stepped in and sort of turned that idea

00:21:16.850 --> 00:21:25.240
on its head. He is so supportive of Gabby
and I think probably the best pieces, his

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:35.040
sense of humor. He the two of them really
have this wonderful dynamic, but he, you know,

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:40.520
if something comes out wrong, that is not
something that is like that. There's nothing

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:46.320
off limits as far as making jokes about things
and they both tease each other. It's just

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:52.039
it's fabulous to watch. She has a really great
sense of humor and in addition to that she

00:21:52.039 --> 00:21:57.210
has a wonderful singing voice. And I wanted
to ask a little bit about music and singing

00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:01.740
and how significant that's been specifically
for her recovery. And I'm curious if you can

00:22:01.740 --> 00:22:07.630
explain a little bit about how music plays
a role in the lives of individuals with Aphasia.

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:15.500
Um and specifically for Gabby, how you incorporated
that into her plan of care. Yeah, so there

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:25.580
are some speech pathology interventions that
rely on music intonation and rhythm and I

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:30.740
know that early in Gabby's rehabilitation
when she was still in Houston. They were doing

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:37.510
some work just from seeing it in the film
um using those approaches, those melodic types

00:22:37.510 --> 00:22:47.520
of approaches. And we didn't do a lot of that
early on when we started working together

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:53.600
because she had a lot of the kind of meaningful
or functional communication without needing

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:59.400
to use those types of approaches. I typically
use those for folks who really struggle with

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:09.400
each word. Um but it we found out as we started
working on speeches and longer and longer

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:19.440
speeches that incorporated a lot of more technical
vocabulary, a lot of multi syllable words

00:23:19.440 --> 00:23:26.080
that if there were words or phrases that were
difficult for her to say, sometimes we could

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:34.529
incorporate some intonation and some rhythm
to help her master those lines. So we have

00:23:34.529 --> 00:23:41.770
sort of come full circle really and started
incorporating rhythm and um kind of melody

00:23:41.770 --> 00:23:53.340
and um intonation into uh speech practice.
But I think even more than that music is just

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:59.279
something that has always been a really important
part of Gabby's life and continues to be part

00:23:59.279 --> 00:24:04.820
of it. So we'll have times during a therapy
session where we will just burst into song

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:11.490
or if we need a break from a really intensive
session, will just sing and I try to not sing

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:20.570
so loud because her voice is fabulous, but
it's such a, you know, if it's a release for

00:24:20.570 --> 00:24:28.090
her, but there's such joy in her face and
in her voice when she sings. I think it's

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:32.960
really good for her soul, even beyond what
it might be doing for speech and language

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:42.169
skills, it just lights up her face and yeah,
it's such a key part of just her life, not

00:24:42.169 --> 00:24:48.200
just her rehabilitation, it sounds like it's
very freeing for her in much the same way

00:24:48.200 --> 00:24:54.510
that I imagine jumping out of a plane is which
